# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.metalvertexdesc).
#
# WHAT THIS BOOT IS FOR. On 21 October the phone's own window server reached our Metal driver
# and asked it for render pipelines. One specification was refused 80 times -- by us, in our
# own words (the 120 lines of that log are 80 of QuartzCore's and 40 of launchd's; 10.45):
#
#   [Metal] Metal failed to build render pipeline: spec=PBGRABsovXm_Tds4A2Xhf_Isrc
#   downsample_blur_vert_lph reads vertex attributes ([[stage_in]]), and this contract
#   carries no vertex attribute layout (rwa_render_shape has no formats, strides or offsets)
#
# That sentence no longer exists in the library. rwa_render_shape now carries
# MTLVertexDescriptor -- a format, an offset and a buffer index per attribute, a stride, a step
# function and a step rate per buffer index -- the driver reads both of Metal's arrays off the
# pipeline descriptor, and rwa_draw carries the streams with their offsets (docs/metal.md 10.44).
# So this boot asks one question: WHAT DOES THE WINDOW SERVER DO NOW. Either those pipelines
# build, or another refusal of ours becomes the one that names itself -- and the list below says
# which sentences are the candidates, in the order the library would reach them, so the log can
# be read against a prediction instead of being read for the first time.
#
# MEASURED ON THIS MACHINE FIRST, and the numbers are in docs/metal.md 10.44:
# air_metal_test --draw-attrib draws QuartzCore's std_vert0_lpf over
# AppleMetalGLRenderer's texturedFragment2DUInt with a real layout -- two attributes out of one
# interleaved stream at buffer index 5, the position NOT at offset 0 -- and every pixel of the
# 4x4 attachment holds the texel its own interpolated coordinate lands in; the same four
# vertices re-encoded as UChar4Normalized and Short4Normalized at another stride and other
# offsets give THE SAME 64 words; and with the elements built in Metal's attribute order instead
# of the shader's driver_location order, all sixteen pixels come back (100, 200, 300, 400)
# instead -- the texel the module's UNUSED attribute 1 points at. 13 bad layouts and 3 bad draws
# are refused by name. air_metal_test --attrib-sweep then builds a pipeline for every function
# of QuartzCore's library: 10 of its 14 [[stage_in]] vertex modules get one, including
# downsample_blur_vert_lph itself, and the other 4 are not refused -- softpipe asserts on the
# 16-bit opcode a `half` attribute narrows with, which is softpipe's limit and the reason this
# half has to be run on the phone at all.
#
# WHAT IT NEEDS
#
#   the image          CRYPTEXES=flash SYSTEM=1 GL=1 REST=1 QUIET=1 \
#                        RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.metalvertexdesc ./build.sh
#                      GL=1 for /redwood/lib/libredwoodair.dylib (the compiler library, which
#                      must be rebuilt from this tree: air_metal.h grew two arrays in the middle
#                      of rwa_render_shape and the driver's own copy grew with them -- a driver
#                      built against one shape and a library built against the other read a
#                      stride where a pixel format is). REST=1 because the window server is the
#                      thing being measured here and it is one of the jobs REST brings.
#   XL_CMDLINE         "bootcpu=7 cpus=8 cpufreq=1 touchscreen=1 gpu=1 metal=1"
#                      gpu=1 or there is no Adreno and every pipeline is refused with a readable
#                      reason instead of being built; metal=1 for the registry properties
#                      metal_props sets below.
#   XL_SECS=420        the window server takes its time, and the pipelines it builds come after
#                      it has composed its first frame.
#
# WHAT THE LOG MUST HOLD, AND BOTH OUTCOMES WRITTEN DOWN BEFORE THE RUN
#
#   grep -a -c "carries no vertex attribute layout"
#     must be 0        That string is gone from the library. Anything but 0 means the image
#                      carries an older libredwoodair.dylib than this driver, and NOTHING else in
#                      this log is about the new code.
#
#   grep -a "vertex descriptor configures"
#     expected         one line per pipeline whose descriptor configures anything:
#                      "redwoodmetal: this pipeline's vertex descriptor configures 3 attributes,
#                      the highest from buffer index 0". QuartzCore's stage-in modules declare
#                      attributes 0, 1 and 3 (position float4, texcoord0 float2, color half4),
#                      so 2 or 3 attributes a pipeline is the shape to expect.
#     if absent        the window server built no pipeline with a vertexDescriptor at all, and
#                      then that specification was refused for something this change does
#                      not touch -- read the refusals below instead.
#
#   grep -a "Metal failed to build render pipeline"
#     the good outcome none, or far fewer than 120.
#     the candidates, in the order rwa_render_create reaches them, each a sentence of ours:
#       "reads vertex attribute N (NAME, TYPE) and the vertex descriptor configures no format
#        there"            -- the descriptor leaves an attribute the module reads unconfigured,
#                             and the module does not gate that attribute with a function
#                             constant. If this is what comes back, the next thing to measure is
#                             WHICH attribute: QuartzCore's fixed_vert_* gate three of their five
#                             with vtx_attr_has_texcoords and vtx_attr_has_point_size, and those
#                             three are allowed to be unconfigured (they get a placeholder
#                             element out of the library's own quad buffer).
#       "is half4, which wants floating-point components, and MTLVertexFormat... delivers
#        signed integer ones"
#                          -- the descriptor's format and the shader's member disagree in base
#                             type. Nothing in QuartzCore should produce this: all 46 of its
#                             attributes are float, float2, float4 or half4.
#       "format 41 is MTLVertexFormatUInt1010102Normalized" (or 40, 54, 55)
#                          -- a packed format, which this library declines because Metal's
#                             channel order against Gallium's is unmeasured here. If the window
#                             server really uses one, that measurement is the next piece of work
#                             and it is not large.
#       "is at offset 4096, and a6xx's VFD_DECODE_INSTR.offset is twelve bits"
#       "has a stride of 4096 bytes"
#                          -- a vertex element bigger than the hardware's twelve-bit fields.
#                             Unlikely for a compositor; it would mean a real limit and not a
#                             mistake.
#       "buffer index K is a vertex stream in this descriptor and FN reads it as [[buffer(K)]]"
#                          -- the one refusal here that is about METAL and not about the
#                             hardware: one table serves both, so an index cannot be both. If
#                             this comes back, the application is asking for something Metal
#                             itself would refuse, and the interesting part is which index.
#       "would not compile the vertex stage"
#                          -- NEW GROUND. That pipeline was refused before ir3 ever saw
#                             their vertex module, so this boot is the first time ir3 compiles
#                             one of them in this driver. docs/air.md says all 135 translatable
#                             QuartzCore modules do compile through ir3 on their own, so this
#                             should not appear; if it does, it is a compiler report and not a
#                             contract one.
#
#   grep -a "the draw failed"
#     past the pipeline is where the new risks are, and they are the draw's:
#       "the vertex descriptor fetches from buffer index K and nothing is bound there"
#                          -- the pipeline fetches a stream the encoder never got. Says the slot.
#       "vertex N of stream K ends X bytes into a buffer that holds Y"
#                          -- the bounds check. Worth reading twice if it appears: the arithmetic
#                             is offset + stride x (highest vertex index) + the attribute's own
#                             size, and a draw refused here that an application believes legal
#                             would mean this check is too strict rather than the draw wrong.
#       "the buffer bound as vertex stream K did not come from the compiler library"
#                          -- a stream from somewhere other than -newBufferWithLength:, which on
#                             this device should be impossible.
#       "vertex buffer K is an argument of the vertex stage and is bound at offset Z"
#                          -- a non-zero offset on a [[buffer(n)]] argument, which rwa_bindings
#                             still has no room for. The STREAM side honours offsets now; this is
#                             the half that does not, and it says so instead of reading the wrong
#                             bytes.
#
#   and the one outcome that is not a sentence: THE LOG STOPPING. A draw that fetches attributes
#   is the first thing this port has ever asked the Adreno's vertex fetch unit to do with a
#   layout of somebody else's making. If the log ends in the middle of a draw, read it as a hung
#   3D core (docs/metal.md 10.36 has the half-GX reset behind a boot-arg) and not as a quiet
#   success.
#
# 1. THE CANARY FIRST, and it is not decoration: clearFragment20 into a 4x4 RGBA32Uint
#    attachment must print the same four words it has printed since 10.22 --
#    2458616957 2930221209 3401825461 3873429713 -- and "THE SAME". This draw uses the
#    GENERATED vertex stage and therefore the library's own quad and its own single vertex
#    element, which is the path 255 pixel-compared draws went through. If it has changed, the
#    layout work broke the road it was supposed to leave alone, and nothing below is worth
#    reading.
/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test --draw /System/Library/Extensions/AppleMetalGLRenderer.bundle/default.metallib
#
# 2. THE NEW REFUSAL, BY NAME, from a program of ours rather than from the window server:
#    metal_drv_test builds a pipeline with a supplied vertex function and NO vertexDescriptor
#    (it has no way to set one), so a module that reads attributes must now be refused for the
#    descriptor and not for the contract. The module is the very one the window server named.
#
#    expected: "reads vertex attribute 0 (position, float4) and the vertex descriptor configures
#    no format there, so the fetch would read a slot nothing was set up for" -- which is the
#    whole of what changed, in one line: the refusal moved from "this contract cannot express a
#    layout" to "this descriptor has not got one", and the attribute's NAME and Apple's own type
#    string come out of the module's metadata on the device.
#
#    if it still says "carries no vertex attribute layout": the image's libredwoodair.dylib is
#    older than this driver and the two must be rebuilt together.
/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test --draw --vertex downsample_blur_vert_lph --fragment upsample_frag_lph /System/Library/Frameworks/QuartzCore.framework/default.metallib
#
# 3. And the same for a module whose attributes are GATED by function constants
#    (fixed_vert_lph_spc: five attributes, three of them behind vtx_attr_has_texcoords and
#    vtx_attr_has_point_size). Two of its five are not gated, so this must still be refused --
#    and the sentence must name one of THOSE two (position, float4, or color, half4) and never a
#    gated one, because a pipeline whose constants make the predicate false may legally configure
#    nothing at that index. A refusal naming texcoord0, texcoord1 or point_size would mean the
#    runtime is demanding a binding the API does not owe it.
/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test --draw --vertex fixed_vert_lph_spc --fragment upsample_frag_lph /System/Library/Frameworks/QuartzCore.framework/default.metallib
#
# 4. The switch, so that iOS's own daemons take the Metal road and the window server reaches
#    this driver (10.14, rc.jobs.metalprops): metal_props puts MetalPluginName and
#    MetalPluginClassName on the IOAcceleratorES service, and from that moment every process
#    that asks Metal composes RedwoodMetal.bundle. Nothing persists -- the properties live in the
#    registry, and a boot without metal=1 is a clean machine.
/redwood/bin/metal_props on
#
# 5. and the class sweep from a process that asks Metal the way the daemons do, which is here
#    for one reason: it proves the chain is alive in THIS boot before the window server's own
#    lines are read as evidence about it (10.17, 10.37).
/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test --sweep
